        "abstract": "The eddy current is one of the unavoidable disadvantages of electrical machines, and the derivation of its calculation and results is not easily achieved. In particular, the eddy current by the magnetic field induces the second magnetic field, and it results in the induced eddy current according to the excitation frequency. In order to study the induced eddy current, this paper uses the Finite Difference Method (FDM) as the numerical method by applying the finite-difference stencil of the curl, div, and discrete Laplace operator. This method is a simple, accurate technique for solving partial differential equations. However, the FDM requires a square mesh, and this requirement has limitations for solving complex shapes, and it increases computation time by making all shapes into fine meshes for calculating the induced eddy current density. To solve this problem, this paper proposes the FDM with a non-uniform mesh to evaluate the eddy current generated at the edges of a structure requiring a fine mesh, which also contributes to the reduction of computing cost. In addition, to ensure the continuity between different size meshes in the non-uniform mesh, the multi-linear interpolation is applied. The numerical results from the non-uniform mesh are compared with a commercial FEM tool and analytical method to demonstrate the accuracy of the results.",
